{-
   What `deriving` refuses on a qualified alias - Analysis-Derive.md §3.

   Every case is a condition the forwarding rewrite is well defined under, reported at the class
   name in the clause rather than from inside an expansion - which is the whole of what §3's "where
   errors land" section asks for. `DeriveNewtype.yana` is the accepting side. This file used to be
   this stage's alone, because the old shipping stage worded its report differently.

   What is deliberately *not* here is "the content cannot do this at all". The old shipping stage
   pre-checked that and reported one message; this stage lets the generated body report, because the
   body is ordinary source written at the clause's own location and a second copy of overload
   resolution here would be a rule that can disagree with the one that decides.
-}

import Atomic

-- A generic newtype, whose instance head would need the context worked out from its content.
alias qualified Wrapped(a) = a deriving (Eq)

-- A class with more than one type variable: there is no single "the content" to forward to.
alias qualified Bits = I64 deriving (Bitcast)

-- The class is not one.
alias qualified Score = Int deriving (Nonexistent)

-- A classification the compiler makes from the type, which an instance may not claim.
alias qualified Word = Int deriving (AtomicValue)

class Wrapping(a):
    fn wrap(x: a) -> Maybe(a)

class Lending(a):
    fn lend(x: 'a) -> 'a

class Stepping(a):
    iter fn steps(x: a) -> Int

-- The class variable does not occur bare. There is no coercion from `Maybe(Ticket)` to `Maybe(I64)`
-- for a forwarding body to emit - Analysis-Derive.md §2's sixth point, which is Haskell's
-- `GeneralizedNewtypeDeriving` restriction written down as a condition.
alias qualified Ticket = I64 deriving (Wrapping)

-- A member that answers a borrow. Putting the name back on one would hand back a borrow of the
-- content under a name for the whole.
alias qualified Lent = I64 deriving (Lending)

-- And one that hands over to a continuation rather than answering a value.
alias qualified Stepped = I64 deriving (Stepping)

fn main() -> Int = 0
